  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe upper level trough has moved out of the region and a ridge is \nnow over the Southeast into the Ohio Valley. At the surface, high \npressure is over the Southeast. This pattern will hold through \nThursday resulting in above normal temperatures and dry weather. \nTemperatures will be about 10 to 13 degrees above normal with a \nstrong ridge in place. With mostly clear skies tonight and calm \nwinds, patchy fog development is likely. With residual ground \nmoisture remaining in many places, fog may be widespread. Guidance \nis mixed on dense fog potential. \n\nBy late afternoon or evening on Thursday, low rain chances start to \nreturn to the northern higher terrain areas as an upper level trough \nmoves through the Midwest and a surface low develops near IL/IN. The \nsurface low will track northeast through the Great Lakes bringing a \ncold front through the Tennessee Valley on Friday. Rain chances will \nbe low for most of the region with this front. The best rain chances \nwill be in Southwest Virginia and the higher elevations. \n\nSaturday and Sunday will be dry and much cooler as a trough moves \nover the Eastern U.S. On Saturday, highs will be mainly in the lower \n60s throughout the Tennessee Valley which is about 10 degrees below \nnormal. Westerly winds behind the cold front will be gusty late \nFriday through Saturday with gusts up to 30-35 mph possible at\ntimes.",
